#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175679 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/id #uuid "7a8775a6-f16c-5fc8-865b-89728d73a2c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-213" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$s(t) = -\\frac{1}{3}t^3 + \\frac{5}{2}t^2 + 6t + 1,\\quad t\\geq 0$.\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary, and give the displacement of the particle from the origin at this time." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{ds}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(-\\frac{1}{3}t^{3} + \\frac{5}{2}t^{2} + 6t + 1) \\\\\n&=-t^{2} + 5t + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when\n\n$$\nv=0\\, \\textrm{m/s}\n$$\n\n. So, we set $v = 0$ and solve for $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= -t^{2} + 5t + 6 \\\\\n0 &= t^{2} - 5t - 6 \\\\\n0 &= (t - 6)(t + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=6$ and $t=-1$. Since\n\n$$\nt\\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe discard the solution $t=-1$ and keep only $t=6$.\n\nSo the particle is stationary only at\n\n$$\nt=6\\, \\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nTo find the displacement from the origin at this time, we substitute $t=6$ back into $s(t)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns(6) &= -\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 6^{3} + \\frac{5}{2} \\cdot 6^{2} + 6 \\cdot 6 + 1 \\\\\n&=-72 + 90 + 36 + 1 \\\\\n&= 55m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at\n\n$$\nt = 6\\, \\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nand its displacement from the origin at this time is $55\\, \\textrm{m}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175680 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "a6a4547d-a81a-5c9a-9ab1-2bb20d847fca" :question/math-academy-id "e-217" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n$s(t) = t^3-3t^2+12t,\\quad t\\geq 0$.\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{ds}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3} - 3t^{2} + 12t) \\\\\n&= 3t^{2} - 6t + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(3t^{2} - 6t + 12) \\\\\n&= 6t - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= 6t - 6 \\\\\n6t &= 6 \\\\\nt &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1$. To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1) = 3(1)^2 - 6(1)+12 = 9\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175712 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/id #uuid "95ddf955-ea59-5ac8-977d-767e46b92bf2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6159" :question/problem "The displacement $s$, in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t) = t^4 - 32t$, where $t \\geq 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$." :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x$, then its velocity is negative.\n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{ds}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{4} - 32t) \\\\\n&= 4t^{3} - 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t) < 0$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}v(t) & < 0 \\\\ 4t^{3} - 32 & < 0 \\\\ 4t^{3} & < 32 \\\\ t^{3} & < 8 \\\\ t & < 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $[MATH: t\\in\\left[0, 2\\right).]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3642872-9487-5302-8cd6-43314b0b78cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = e^{-3/2}, v =-2e^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175459} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ad42bb-06cc-5675-9192-87736ec8566e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = e^{-5/2}, v =-e^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175460} {:answer/id #uuid "f9cdb155-39ad-5ef3-9905-e8e0a468e6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = e^{-3/2}, v =-e^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175461} {:answer/id #uuid "3354c4fb-c827-5c6e-adb4-d04af78f599a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = e^{-1/2}, v =-2e^{-1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175462} {:answer/id #uuid "6241a30d-ffed-548d-988f-d84d4b829de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = e^{-5/2}, v =-2e^{-5/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3642872-9487-5302-8cd6-43314b0b78cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = e^{-3/2}, v =-2e^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175459} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8d9b94d-adb8-5a4a-93bd-d684f585cbb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175458}] :question/id #uuid "f4d760c3-ed2b-5b02-959e-0a9b0b6ecac1" :question/math-academy-id "q-73334" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n$s(t) = t^{2}\\ln (t), t \\ge 0$.\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
